====================================================
SAGUARO AI HEALTH MONITORING — SAM TESTING GUIDE
====================================================
Written by: Afriha
Purpose: Test SAM (Segment Anything Model) on saguaro cactus images
         to verify it can isolate the cactus from the background.

----------------------------------------------------
WHAT WE ARE TESTING
----------------------------------------------------
We are checking if SAM can look at a photo of a saguaro and
correctly outline just the cactus — ignoring the background,
shrubs, sky, etc.

A good result = the blue mask covers the cactus body and not
much else. A bad result = it misses big chunks of the cactus
or includes a lot of background.

Please note down what you observe after each test.

----------------------------------------------------
STEP 1: INSTALL PYTHON
----------------------------------------------------
If you don't have Python installed:
1. Go to https://www.python.org/downloads/
2. Download the latest version for your computer
3. During install, CHECK the box that says "Add Python to PATH"
4. Click Install Now

To verify it worked, open Command Prompt
and type:
    python --version

You should see something like: Python 3.11.x
If not, remove Python and reinstall it and make sure to add it to your PATH 
----------------------------------------------------
STEP 2: INSTALL REQUIRED PACKAGES
----------------------------------------------------
Open Command Prompt and run these one at a time:

    pip install torch
    pip install ultralytics

These will take a few minutes to download. 

----------------------------------------------------
STEP 3: SET UP YOUR FILES
----------------------------------------------------
1. Create a folder on your Desktop called: EPICS_SAM_Test or whatever you want just for the sake of easy replication use what I used 
2. Save your cactus image inside that folder
   - Name it: testing.png again you can change the naming 
   - Use a clear photo of a single saguaro cactus
   - JPG or PNG both work, just update the file extension in the
     script if needed

----------------------------------------------------
STEP 4: CREATE THE SCRIPT
----------------------------------------------------
1. Open Notepad or VS Code, if you have it. For VS Code open it to the EPCIS_SAM_Testing folder and do the following 
2. Copy and paste the following code exactly:

------ COPY FROM HERE ------

from ultralytics import SAM

print("Loading model... (first time will download weights, be patient)")
model = SAM("sam2_b.pt")
print("Model loaded!")

image_path = r"C:\Users\YOUR_USERNAME\Desktop\EPICS_SAM_Test\testing.png"

print("Running SAM on image...")
results = model(image_path, points=[350, 350], labels=[1])
print("Done! Showing result...")

results[0].show()
print("Finished!")

------ COPY TO HERE ------

3. IMPORTANT: Replace YOUR_USERNAME with your actual Windows/Mac
   username in the image_path line. For example if your name
   is Bob it would be:
   r"C:\Users\Bob\Desktop\EPICS_SAM_Test\testing.png"

4. Save the file as: SAM_testing.py
   - In Notepad, go File > Save As
   - Change "Save as type" to "All Files"
   - Name it SAM_testing.py
   - Save it inside your EPICS_SAM_Test folder

NOTE: If you didn't name your folder or image the same name as I did, you change it on the image_path 
----------------------------------------------------
STEP 5: RUN THE SCRIPT
----------------------------------------------------
1. Open Command Prompt
2. Navigate to your folder by typing:
       cd Desktop\EPICS_SAM_Test
3. Run the script:
       python SAM_testing.py
Or if there is another way, please feel free to use it! 

First time running: it will download the SAM2 model weights
automatically. This is a large file, just let it run.

A window should pop up showing your cactus image with a blue
mask overlay on it. There will also be a confidence score
shown (e.g. 0.97 = 97% confident).

----------------------------------------------------
STEP 6: WHAT TO RECORD
----------------------------------------------------
After each image test, write down:

- Image description (e.g. "overcast day,lighting is bad")
- Confidence score shown (the number next to the blue box)
- Did the mask cover the full cactus? (Yes / Mostly / No)
- Did the mask include background? (None / A little / A lot)
- Any other notes (e.g. "missed the arms", "caught nearby shrub")

----------------------------------------------------
TROUBLESHOOTING
----------------------------------------------------
"No such file or directory" error:
  -> Double check your image_path username and filename are correct

"SyntaxError: unicode error" error:
  -> Make sure you have the r before the quotes in image_path
     It should look like: r"C:\Users\..."

Script runs but no window appears:
  -> Try adding this at the very end of the script:
     input("Press Enter to exit...")
     This keeps the window open

pip is not recognized:
  -> Python was not added to PATH during install
  -> Reinstall Python and check the "Add to PATH" box

If any other question, you can ask me or use any AI for troubleshooting!

----------------------------------------------------
NOTES
----------------------------------------------------
- The point [350, 350] is a coordinate in the middle of the
  image that tells SAM where to look. If your cactus is not
  centered, you may need to adjust this.
- x=0 is the LEFT edge of the image
- y=0 is the TOP edge of the image
- So [350, 350] means 350 pixels from the left, 350 from the top

HOW TO FIND THE RIGHT COORDINATES FOR YOUR IMAGE:
1. Open your cactus image in Paint (search in Start menu)
2. Hover your mouse over the middle of the cactus trunk
3. Look at the bottom of the Paint window — it shows the
   coordinates of your cursor, e.g. "347, 512px"
4. Use those two numbers in the script:
       results = model(image_path, points=[347, 512], labels=[1])
5. Pick a point clearly on the trunk, not on the arms or edges
Or again just put it into GEMINI or CHAT and it for coordinates
